# The Plucky Squire (PPSA15319): PAD-READ anchored route to chapter-one gameplay.
#
# Why read anchors ('p') and not wall-clock seconds: this title's guest tick rate is not
# constant and does not vary by a little.  Measured on master 9dcf807f, one run, native 4K:
#
#     logos + title + SAVE FILES + PLAY STYLE   ~25   guest polls/s
#     Desk_C01 streaming                        ~4    guest polls/s
#     desk level up, pre-cutscene               ~2.1  guest polls/s
#     chapter-one intro cutscene                ~0.17 guest polls/s
#
# A 147x spread means one wall-clock cadence cannot serve both ends: the 5 s pulses that pace
# the menus correctly deliver only ~1 poll each once the 3D world is up, and a wall-clock gap
# that is comfortable in a menu is over ten minutes of cutscene.  Pad-read anchors are the
# guest's own axis, so every window below is worth a fixed number of polls no matter how long
# the host takes to serve them.
#
# The menu block mirrors the proven wall-clock route (scripts/plucky-squire/reach-first-gameplay.pad)
# in read space: its eight Cross presses landed at reads 595/1178/1680/2170/2658/3136/3594/4052.
# Cross is the game's own UI_Select and Jump (Gamepad_FaceButton_Bottom), read out of the title's
# InputSettingsKeyMappings save, so it is both the menu confirm and a harmless gameplay input.
p500-625:cross
p1000-1125:cross
p1500-1625:cross
p2000-2125:cross
p2500-2625:cross
p3000-3125:cross
p3500-3625:cross
p4000-4125:cross
#
# World block: dense enough to matter at 0.17 polls/s.  Six-poll windows every 40 polls, so a
# press is always seen and released across at least one full guest tick.
